---
import Page from "@/layouts/Page.astro";
import NeoCard from "@/components/neobrutalist/NeoCard.astro";
import StickyNote from "@/components/neobrutalist/StickyNote.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "@/components/neobrutalist/ComparisonTable.astro";
import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
import { SITE } from "@/consts";
import { breadcrumbList, faqPage } from "@/lib/seo";

const breadcrumbLd = breadcrumbList([
  { name: "Home", url: SITE.URL },
  { name: "Tools", url: "/tools/" },
  { name: "Server Capacity Calculator", url: "/tools/capacity-calculator/" },
]);

const DESCRIPTION =
  "Free server capacity calculator. Little's Law sizing: turn requests/sec, latency, and concurrency into the worker count you actually need — in your browser.";

// Concurrency numbers are what each runtime does by default, not what it can be
// tuned to. Latencies are placeholders so the preset produces a whole scenario.
const PRESETS = [
  {
    label: "Laravel Octane worker",
    note: "Swoole / FrankenPHP, one request per worker process",
    concurrency: "1",
    latency: "40",
  },
  {
    label: "Node single process",
    note: "Event loop, I/O-bound handlers, nothing CPU-heavy",
    concurrency: "40",
    latency: "120",
  },
  {
    label: "Python sync WSGI worker",
    note: "Gunicorn sync worker — blocks for the whole request",
    concurrency: "1",
    latency: "80",
  },
  {
    label: "Go goroutine service",
    note: "One goroutine per request, bounded by CPU and pool limits",
    concurrency: "200",
    latency: "25",
  },
];

// Fixed utilization steps for the queueing table. Fill tones are literal strings
// so Tailwind keeps them; nothing here is built from a dynamic class name.
const QUEUE_ROWS = [
  { pct: 50, fill: "bg-green" },
  { pct: 70, fill: "bg-green" },
  { pct: 80, fill: "bg-yellow" },
  { pct: 90, fill: "bg-yellow" },
  { pct: 95, fill: "bg-pink" },
  { pct: 99, fill: "bg-red" },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: "How many servers do I need for X requests per second?",
    a: "Multiply your target requests per second by the average time one request occupies a worker, in seconds. That product is the number of requests in flight at any instant — Little's Law. Divide it by how many requests a single worker handles concurrently, then divide again by your target utilization. At 300 req/s, 120 ms of service time, 40 concurrent slots per worker and a 70% target, that is 300 × 0.12 = 36 in flight, 36 ÷ 40 ÷ 0.7 = 1.3, so 2 workers.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is Little's Law in simple terms?",
    a: "L = λ × W. The average number of items inside a system equals how fast they arrive multiplied by how long each one stays. It holds for any stable system regardless of arrival pattern, service time distribution, or queueing discipline — it is arithmetic, not a model. The catch is that it describes long-run averages, so it tells you the floor on capacity and nothing at all about your worst minute.",
  },
  {
    q: "What utilization should I target for a web service?",
    a: "60-75% for anything user-facing. Queueing delay is proportional to 1/(1-ρ), so the cost of the last few percent is brutal: going from 70% to 90% roughly triples queue wait on a single-slot worker, and 90% to 95% doubles it again. Batch workers and queue consumers can run at 85-90% because nobody is watching a spinner. Anything above 90% on a synchronous path means your p99 is set by queueing, not by your code.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should I size workers with average latency or p99?",
    a: "Size the steady state with average latency, then sanity-check the fleet with p99. Little's Law uses the mean by definition, so the average is the mathematically correct input for the long-run number. But traffic arrives in bursts, and during a burst it is the slow requests that pin workers down — if your average is 80 ms and your p99 is 900 ms, a burst of slow requests occupies eleven times more slots than the average predicts. Run the calculator twice and treat the p99 answer as your burst budget.",
  },
  {
    q: "How many Gunicorn or Octane workers do I need?",
    a: "Both default to one request per worker process, so concurrency per worker is 1 and the answer is simply in-flight requests divided by your target utilization, rounded up. At 50 req/s with 80 ms handlers that is 4 in flight, or 6 workers at 70%. Then cap it by memory and CPU: a process that needs 250 MB resident cannot be scaled to 40 copies on a 4 GB box no matter what the arithmetic says.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does this capacity calculator send my numbers anywhere?",
    a: "No. There is no backend, no analytics call on the inputs, and no network request of any kind after the page loads. Every number — Little's Law, the worker counts, and the Erlang C queueing table — is computed in JavaScript inside your tab. Switch the network off and it keeps working.",
  },
];

const appLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
  name: "Server Capacity Calculator",
  applicationCategory: "DeveloperApplication",
  operatingSystem: "Any (browser)",
  url: new URL("/tools/capacity-calculator/", SITE.URL).href,
  description: DESCRIPTION,
  offers: { "@type": "Offer", price: "0", priceCurrency: "USD" },
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  browserRequirements: "Requires JavaScript",
  author: { "@id": `${SITE.URL}#person` },
};

const faqLd = faqPage(faq.map((f) => ({ question: f.q, answer: f.a })));

const inputClass =
  "border-[3px] border-ink rounded-[4px] bg-paper px-3 py-3 font-mono font-bold shadow-neo-sm focus:outline-none";
const selectClass =
  "border-[3px] border-ink rounded-[4px] bg-paper px-3 py-3 font-bold shadow-neo-sm focus:outline-none";
const labelClass =
  "mono text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-widest block mb-2";
const hintClass = "mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest opacity-70 mt-2";
const codeClass =
  "font-mono text-[0.88em] font-bold border-[2px] border-ink rounded-[3px] bg-bg-alt px-1";
const thClass =
  "border-b-[3px] border-r-[3px] border-ink p-3 mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-widest";
const tdClass = "border-b-[2px] border-r-[3px] border-ink p-3 text-sm";
const dtClass = "text-xs font-bold uppercase";
const ddClass = "text-right font-mono text-sm font-bold";
---

<Page
  title="Server Capacity Calculator"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  eyebrow="Free tool"
  heading="Server Capacity Calculator."
  intro="How many workers do you actually need? Enter throughput, latency, and concurrency per worker — Little's Law sizes the fleet, and the queueing table shows what the last 20% of utilization costs you."
  structuredData={[breadcrumbLd, appLd, faqLd]}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-6">
    <StickyNote tone="yellow" tilt={-1.5} class="max-w-md">
      <p class="mono mb-1 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">
        ▸ The law is exact, your inputs aren't
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-snug font-bold">
        L = λ × W holds for any stable system. Everything downstream of it is
        only as honest as the latency and concurrency you feed in.
      </p>
    </StickyNote>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-14" id="capacity-calculator-root">
    <div class="grid items-start gap-6 md:grid-cols-[1fr_360px]">
      <!-- Inputs -->
      <NeoCard tone="paper" shadow="md" class="min-w-0 space-y-5">
        <div>
          <p class={labelClass}>Solve for</p>
          <div
            role="group"
            aria-label="Choose which direction to solve"
            class="border-ink shadow-neo-xs inline-flex overflow-hidden rounded-[4px] border-[3px]"
          >
            <button
              type="button"
              data-capacity-mode="fleet"
              aria-pressed="true"
              class="mono border-ink bg-yellow border-r-[3px] px-3.5 py-2 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase transition-colors"
            >
              Workers needed
            </button>
            <button
              type="button"
              data-capacity-mode="load"
              aria-pressed="false"
              class="mono bg-paper px-3.5 py-2 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase transition-colors"
            >
              Max throughput
            </button>
          </div>
          <p class={hintClass}>
            ▸ Same law, both directions. Pick the one you don't know yet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity-calculator-rate-field">
          <label for="capacity-calculator-rate" class={labelClass}>
            Target throughput
          </label>
          <div class="flex gap-3">
            <input
              id="capacity-calculator-rate"
              type="number"
              min="0"
              step="any"
              inputmode="decimal"
              value="120"
              autocomplete="off"
              class={`${inputClass} min-w-0 flex-1`}
            />
            <select
              id="capacity-calculator-rate-unit"
              aria-label="Throughput unit"
              class={`${selectClass} w-28 shrink-0 sm:w-36`}
            >
              <option value="sec" selected>req / sec</option>
              <option value="min">req / min</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <p class={hintClass}>
            ▸ Peak, not daily average. Divide the busiest hour by 3,600.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity-calculator-workers-field" hidden>
          <label for="capacity-calculator-workers" class={labelClass}>
            Workers / pods you have
          </label>
          <input
            id="capacity-calculator-workers"
            type="number"
            min="1"
            step="1"
            inputmode="numeric"
            value="6"
            autocomplete="off"
            class={`${inputClass} w-full`}
          />
          <p class={hintClass}>
            ▸ Processes, pods, containers — whatever your concurrency figure is
            per.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <label for="capacity-calculator-latency" class={labelClass}>
            Average latency (ms)
          </label>
          <input
            id="capacity-calculator-latency"
            type="number"
            min="0"
            step="any"
            inputmode="decimal"
            value="200"
            autocomplete="off"
            class={`${inputClass} w-full`}
          />
          <p class={hintClass}>
            ▸ Server-side handler time, measured at low load. Not the number
            your users see during a spike.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <label for="capacity-calculator-concurrency" class={labelClass}>
            Concurrency per worker
          </label>
          <input
            id="capacity-calculator-concurrency"
            type="number"
            min="1"
            step="1"
            inputmode="numeric"
            value="6"
            autocomplete="off"
            class={`${inputClass} w-full`}
          />
          <p class={hintClass}>
            ▸ How many requests one worker holds at once. Sync PHP/Python
            workers: 1.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <label for="capacity-calculator-utilization" class={labelClass}>
            Target utilization (%)
          </label>
          <input
            id="capacity-calculator-utilization"
            type="number"
            min="1"
            max="99"
            step="1"
            inputmode="numeric"
            value="70"
            autocomplete="off"
            class={`${inputClass} w-full`}
          />
          <p class={hintClass}>
            ▸ 60-75% for user-facing traffic. 85-90% only for queue consumers.
          </p>
        </div>
      </NeoCard>

      <!-- Output -->
      <NeoCard
        tone="cyan"
        shadow="lg"
        class="min-w-0 space-y-4 md:sticky md:top-24"
      >
        <p class="mono text-[11px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">
          ▸ <span id="capacity-calculator-headline-label">Workers needed</span>
        </p>

        <div
          id="capacity-calculator-results"
          aria-live="polite"
          class="space-y-4"
        >
          <div>
            <p
              id="capacity-calculator-headline"
              class="display text-accent-text text-5xl leading-none"
            >
              —
            </p>
            <p
              id="capacity-calculator-headline-sub"
              class="mono mt-2 text-[11px] tracking-widest uppercase opacity-80"
            >
              —
            </p>
          </div>

          <dl class="border-ink space-y-2.5 border-t-[2.5px] pt-3">
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class={dtClass}>In flight (L = λ × W)</dt>
              <dd id="capacity-calculator-inflight" class={ddClass}>—</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class={dtClass}>Slots in the pool</dt>
              <dd id="capacity-calculator-slots" class={ddClass}>—</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class={dtClass} id="capacity-calculator-alt-label">
                Workers at 100%
              </dt>
              <dd id="capacity-calculator-alt" class={ddClass}>—</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class={dtClass}>Real utilization</dt>
              <dd id="capacity-calculator-actual-util" class={ddClass}>—</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class={dtClass}>Headroom before saturation</dt>
              <dd id="capacity-calculator-headroom" class={ddClass}>—</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class={dtClass}>Throughput per worker</dt>
              <dd id="capacity-calculator-per-worker" class={ddClass}>—</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>

        <div
          id="capacity-calculator-error"
          role="status"
          hidden
          class="border-ink bg-red rounded-[4px] border-[3px] px-3 py-2.5"
        >
          <p class="mono mb-1 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">
            ▸ Check the inputs
          </p>
          <p
            id="capacity-calculator-error-text"
            class="text-sm leading-snug font-bold"
          >
          </p>
        </div>

        <button
          type="button"
          id="capacity-calculator-copy"
          class="border-ink bg-paper shadow-neo-sm hover:bg-yellow hover:shadow-neo-md inline-flex w-full items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-[6px] border-[3px] px-4 py-2.5 text-xs font-bold tracking-wide uppercase transition-all hover:-translate-x-0.5 hover:-translate-y-0.5 disabled:opacity-50"
        >
          <Icon name="lucide:copy" class="h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true" />
          <span id="capacity-calculator-copy-label">Copy sizing</span>
        </button>

        <p class="mono text-[10px] tracking-widest uppercase opacity-70">
          Runs in your browser · nothing uploaded
        </p>
      </NeoCard>
    </div>

    <!-- Presets -->
    <h2 class="heading mt-10 mb-3 text-xl uppercase">
      Runtime starting points.
    </h2>
    <div
      class="border-ink bg-paper shadow-neo-md overflow-x-auto rounded-[4px] border-[3px]"
    >
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
        <caption class="sr-only">
          Default concurrency and a plausible latency for four common runtimes
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-yellow">
            <th scope="col" class={thClass}>Runtime</th>
            <th scope="col" class={thClass}>Concurrency</th>
            <th scope="col" class={thClass}>Latency</th>
            <th
              scope="col"
              class="border-ink mono border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
            >
              Apply
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            PRESETS.map((p, i) => (
              <tr class={i % 2 ? "bg-bg-alt" : "bg-paper"}>
                <th
                  scope="row"
                  class="border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-left align-top font-bold"
                >
                  {p.label}
                  <span class="mono mt-1 block text-[10px] font-normal tracking-widest uppercase opacity-70">
                    {p.note}
                  </span>
                </th>
                <td class={`${tdClass} font-mono font-bold whitespace-nowrap`}>
                  {p.concurrency} / worker
                </td>
                <td class={`${tdClass} font-mono font-bold whitespace-nowrap`}>
                  {p.latency} ms
                </td>
                <td class="border-ink border-b-[2px] p-3">
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    data-preset-concurrency={p.concurrency}
                    data-preset-latency={p.latency}
                    class="mono border-ink bg-bg-alt shadow-neo-xs hover:bg-yellow hover:shadow-neo-sm rounded-full border-[2px] px-3 py-1 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase transition-all hover:-translate-x-0.5 hover:-translate-y-0.5"
                  >
                    Use
                  </button>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class={hintClass}>
      ▸ Starting points, not truths. Every one of these moves with your
      middleware, your database, and your GC settings — measure yours.
    </p>

    <!-- Queueing -->
    <h2 class="heading mt-10 mb-3 text-xl uppercase">
      What the last 20% costs.
    </h2>
    <div
      class="border-ink bg-paper shadow-neo-md overflow-x-auto rounded-[4px] border-[3px]"
    >
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
        <caption class="sr-only">
          Expected queue wait and total response time at rising utilization
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-cyan">
            <th scope="col" class={thClass}>Utilization</th>
            <th scope="col" class={thClass}>Queue wait</th>
            <th scope="col" class={thClass}>Total response</th>
            <th
              scope="col"
              class="border-ink mono border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
            >
              Wait ÷ service time
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="capacity-calculator-queue-body" aria-live="polite">
          {
            QUEUE_ROWS.map((row, i) => (
              <tr class={i % 2 ? "bg-bg-alt" : "bg-paper"}>
                <th
                  scope="row"
                  class="border-ink mono border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-left font-bold whitespace-nowrap"
                >
                  {row.pct}%
                </th>
                <td
                  id={`capacity-calculator-q-${row.pct}-wait`}
                  class={`${tdClass} font-mono font-bold whitespace-nowrap`}
                >
                  —
                </td>
                <td
                  id={`capacity-calculator-q-${row.pct}-total`}
                  class={`${tdClass} font-mono font-bold whitespace-nowrap`}
                >
                  —
                </td>
                <td class="border-ink min-w-[180px] border-b-[2px] p-3">
                  <span
                    id={`capacity-calculator-q-${row.pct}-mult`}
                    class="mono mb-1.5 block text-[11px] font-bold"
                  >
                    —
                  </span>
                  <span class="border-ink bg-paper block h-2.5 overflow-hidden rounded-[2px] border-[2px]">
                    <span
                      id={`capacity-calculator-q-${row.pct}-bar`}
                      class:list={["block h-full w-0", row.fill]}
                    />
                  </span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p id="capacity-calculator-queue-caption" class={hintClass}>—</p>
    <p class="text-ink/80 mt-3 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <strong>How this is computed.</strong> Exact Erlang C for the M/M/c model:
      <code class={codeClass}>Wq / S = C(c, a) ÷ (c × (1 − ρ))</code>, where
      <code class={codeClass}>a = ρ × c</code> is offered load in erlangs and
      <code class={codeClass}>C</code> comes from the standard Erlang B recursion
      <code class={codeClass}>B(n) = a·B(n−1) ÷ (n + a·B(n−1))</code>. The
      arithmetic is exact. <em>The model is not your traffic.</em> M/M/c assumes Poisson
      arrivals, exponentially distributed service times, one shared queue, infinitely
      patient clients, and zero retries. It also treats every concurrency slot as
      an independent server, which flatters an I/O-bound runtime whose forty slots
      share one CPU. Real bursts are worse than Poisson, real latency has a fatter
      tail, and a retry storm breaks the model outright — so read these numbers as
      the optimistic floor on queueing pain, not a forecast. What they are good for
      is the <em>shape</em>: the ratio between the 70% row and the 95% row is
      the point.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Explainer -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-14">
    <div class="text-ink/90 space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="display text-2xl uppercase">Little's Law, stated plainly</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>L = λ × W.</strong> The average number of requests inside your service
        equals the rate they arrive at, multiplied by how long each one stays. Nothing
        else. If 120 requests per second arrive and each takes 200 ms, then on average
        24 requests are inside your service at any instant — 120 × 0.2. Those 24 have
        to live somewhere: in a worker slot, in a goroutine, on an event loop, or
        in a queue in front of all of them. That is the whole sizing problem, and
        it is the number the tool prints as <code class={codeClass}>L</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The remarkable thing about this law is that it assumes nothing. No
        distribution of arrivals, no distribution of service times, no queueing
        discipline, no independence. Any stable system, over a long enough
        window, obeys it. That makes it the one piece of capacity maths you can
        trust completely — and also the reason it can never tell you about your
        worst minute, because averages have no tails.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tool applies it in both directions.
        <strong>Workers needed</strong> computes L from your throughput and latency,
        divides by concurrency per worker, then divides again by target utilization
        and rounds up. <strong>Max throughput</strong> runs it backwards: your worker
        count times concurrency gives total slots, slots times target utilization
        gives busy slots, and busy slots divided by latency gives the arrival rate
        that keeps you there. Same equation, rearranged.
      </p>

      <h2 class="display pt-4 text-2xl uppercase">
        Why 100% utilization is a trap
      </h2>
      <p>
        The naive answer to "how many workers" is L divided by concurrency. That
        number is correct and useless, because it sizes for a world where
        requests arrive on a metronome. They don't. Arrivals cluster, and when
        two requests want the last free slot at the same time, one of them
        waits.
      </p>
      <p>
        Queueing delay scales with <code class={codeClass}>1 / (1 − ρ)</code>.
        At 50% utilization the term is 2. At 90% it is 10. At 99% it is 100.
        That is why the table above bends the way it does: the first 70% of
        capacity is nearly free, and the last 5% costs more than everything
        before it combined. Utilization is not an efficiency score. Past about
        80% it is a latency dial you are turning the wrong way.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is one genuine mitigation, and the table shows it: pool size. A
        single worker with one slot at 90% utilization queues for nine service
        times. Forty slots behind one queue at the same 90% barely queue at all,
        because a free slot is almost always available somewhere. This is the
        entire argument for a shared queue in front of a worker pool instead of
        per-worker queues, and it is why a load balancer doing
        least-outstanding-requests beats round-robin under load.
      </p>

      <h2 class="display pt-4 text-2xl uppercase">
        p99, not average, decides the fleet
      </h2>
      <p>
        Little's Law wants the mean, so the mean is the right input for the
        steady-state number. It is the wrong input for surviving a burst. A
        service with 80 ms average latency and 900 ms p99 does not occupy
        workers for 80 ms — it occupies them for 80 ms most of the time and 900
        ms exactly when everyone shows up at once. During those seconds the
        effective W is an order of magnitude larger, L jumps with it, and a
        fleet sized on the average has no slots left.
      </p>
      <p>
        Practical approach: run the calculator once with your average to get the
        baseline, once with your p99 to get the burst budget, and put the fleet
        somewhere between them depending on how expensive a queued request is.
        If a slow request holds a database connection or a file handle, size
        closer to p99 — the resource, not the CPU, is what runs out. And if you
        do not know your p99, that is the first thing to fix; a mean latency
        with no percentile behind it is a number you cannot plan on.
      </p>

      <h2 class="display pt-4 text-2xl uppercase">
        Concurrency is not parallelism
      </h2>
      <p>
        The "concurrency per worker" field is the one people get wrong, because
        it means different things depending on what your requests spend their
        time doing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ComparisonTable
      class="mt-6"
      columns={[
        { label: "I/O-bound", tone: "cyan" },
        { label: "CPU-bound", tone: "pink" },
      ]}
      rows={[
        {
          label: "Where time goes",
          cells: [
            "Waiting on DB, HTTP, disk, an LLM",
            "Hashing, parsing, rendering, inference",
          ],
        },
        {
          label: "Concurrency per worker",
          cells: [
            "High — dozens to hundreds of waiting requests",
            "≈ cores available to that worker, and no more",
          ],
        },
        {
          label: "What adding slots does",
          cells: [
            "Absorbs more waiting, latency barely moves",
            "Nothing but queue inside the process",
          ],
        },
        {
          label: "Scale by",
          cells: [
            "Raising concurrency, then adding workers",
            "Adding workers or cores — full stop",
          ],
        },
        {
          label: "Failure signature",
          cells: [
            "Connection pool exhausted downstream",
            "Run queue climbs, every response slows at once",
          ],
        },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="text-ink/90 mt-6 space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Concurrency is how many requests are in progress; parallelism is how
        many are actually executing on a core right now. A Node process can hold
        four hundred concurrent requests and still be running exactly one line
        of your code at a time. That is fine while they are all waiting on
        Postgres, and catastrophic the moment one of them starts parsing a 10 MB
        JSON payload — the event loop stalls and all four hundred stall with it.
        Set concurrency to what the runtime can hold, then check whether your
        handlers actually spend their time waiting. If they don't, the honest
        number is the core count.
      </p>

      <h2 class="display pt-4 text-2xl uppercase">
        Autoscaling doesn't remove the headroom
      </h2>
      <p>
        The most common reason a correctly-sized service falls over is that the
        replacement capacity arrives late. Metrics scrape on an interval, the
        autoscaler evaluates on an interval, a new pod schedules, an image
        pulls, the runtime boots, the JIT warms, connection pools fill, and the
        load balancer's health check has to pass twice. Thirty seconds is a good
        day on Kubernetes with a warm image cache; two to four minutes is normal
        on a cold VM.
      </p>
      <p>
        For that entire window your existing workers absorb the whole surge —
        which is precisely what the target utilization field is buying you.
        Rough rule: your headroom should cover the traffic growth you can see
        during one full scale-out cycle. A service that doubles in 60 seconds
        and takes 90 seconds to add a pod needs to be sitting near 50%, not 70%,
        or it needs a pre-warmed floor of replicas. Scaling on CPU makes this
        worse for I/O-bound services, since CPU stays flat while the request
        queue grows — scale on in-flight requests or queue depth instead, which
        is the L this tool just computed for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        And do not size for the peak you have today. Size for the peak plus one
        failure domain: if a third of your pods live in an availability zone
        that can disappear, the remaining two thirds have to run the whole load
        at an acceptable utilization. That is a multiplier on the number above,
        not a rounding error.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

